        ":question/id": "dccc05e0-874a-5050-930e-06a0a4c404ed",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x y?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-1-policy-2-e0ceec0efc26ec01/assets/17592186172040/plots/4e472f0fcb64d0d53ce1401ad6ee3e134a54837d96da5b899c97c18146c1341e.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-1-policy-2-e0ceec0efc26ec01/assets/17592186172040/plots/045525575c74f776ec759ff440a4c7e1fb994cbf9305e7b2cebb74bce9030cb8.png)",
